          "text": "**Observations:**\n- **Image border.** Row 0 weights stay at initialization size (|w| ≤ about 0.036, the uniform ±1/√784 range), except for a few moderate values in columns 12–15. All other border regions are strongly trained.\n  - Row 1, columns 4–23, has large weights (|w| up to about 0.37).\n  - Row 2 has large weights at both its left part (columns 2–10) and its right part (columns 17–24).\n  - Row 27, columns 5–23, has clearly trained weights (up to about 0.18).\n  - Row 26 has strong weights at columns 4–8 and 21–22 (unit 5 reaches about 0.34).\n- **Side columns.** One column, about column 24, carries very large, consistent weights from about row 3 down to row 22. The columns beyond it (25–27) and columns 0–1 of the middle rows also carry clearly non-zero weights (0.1–0.25). So the whole width of the image is used, not only a central 20×20 box.\n- **Centre.** Several units have compact blobs of large weights in the image interior. Unit 4 has a central column around rows 13–17 (about +0.33). Unit 11 has a blob in rows 18–21 near columns 10–12 (about +0.37). Several units have broad positive regions at the bottom-left.\n- **Hidden units.** The units look like silhouette and extent detectors: whether the object reaches the top, the bottom, the far sides, and the centre. They do not look like stroke or edge detectors.\n- **Output layer.** The 16×10 output weights are fairly entangled. A few units group classes: unit 5 is positive for classes 0, 5, 7 and 9 (strongly 9) and negative for 1, 2 and 4. Unit 13 is negative for 2, 3 and 6 and positive for 1, 4, 7, 8 and 9.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- **Not MNIST or EMNIST.** These datasets have empty borders: rows 0–3 and 24–27 and the outer columns are nearly always zero. Here rows 1–2, rows 26–27 and columns 0–1 and 24–27 are all strongly trained, so the objects reach the frame edges. That rules out MNIST-style centred digits in a 20×20 box.\n- **Fashion-MNIST fits.** Its items are scaled so the longest edge